WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.360
Film Courage: Switching gears just a tiny
bit, have you ever not taken on a project

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:07.210
and if so, for what reasons?

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:08.850
LA4hire’s Wendy Kram: All the time.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:15.370
I mean most of the time and that’s the fact
of what most writers are faced with is that

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:17.950
it is a game of mostly rejections.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:26.320
I know as producer I feel like if I wind up
optioning a project, writers would beat a

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:31.970
lot of odds to have someone decide they want
to option the project.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:33.570
I then have to go and sell it.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:44.739
I had a project once where it took me a year
and I sent it to every single network and

00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:48.710
this was during the time when you had to wait,
the protocol was you couldn’t take it somewhere

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:50.220
else until you heard back.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:56.309
Today you can do multiple submissions but
that was the protocol back then.

00:00:56.309 --> 00:01:01.270
And it was the last network that I took it
to and the head of the network who was very

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:08.270
well-respected industry individual, the executive
showed us his comment.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:15.599
What they do would do was the executives would
every month they would gather the top tiers

00:01:15.599 --> 00:01:19.270
of the projects that they’ve read and liked
and they would give it to their boss.

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:20.659
He would read it over the weekend.

00:01:20.659 --> 00:01:25.319
Our’s came back and we had been rejected
like 12 places but I always knew it was a

00:01:25.319 --> 00:01:30.969
good story and the rejections were “It just
doesn’t fit into our mandate.

00:01:30.969 --> 00:01:34.149
We’re not doing this kind of story right
now.”

00:01:34.149 --> 00:01:39.109
And anyway he wrote in the margin “This
is a good story.”

00:01:39.109 --> 00:01:45.649
So I know an agent who once said that “All
it takes is one yes.”

00:01:45.649 --> 00:01:53.499
Now it’s also a fine balance if you’re
getting No…No…No…No..No consistently

00:01:53.499 --> 00:01:54.960
with feedback.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.329
Like the feedback that I was getting wasn’t
“This isn’t a good story.”

00:01:58.329 --> 00:02:01.799
It was “This isn’t the right project for
us right now.”

00:02:01.799 --> 00:02:04.090
Or “We have something similar to this.”

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:09.329
If you’re getting feedback where someone
says and I’ve heard this quite a bit “I

00:02:09.329 --> 00:02:11.120
like the concept.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.030
I didn’t love the execution.”

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:17.260
And I can elaborate on that.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:21.291
Or “I really liked the concept, but I didn’t
love the protagonist.

00:02:21.291 --> 00:02:24.790
I didn’t feel that character was developed
enough.”

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:30.370
And that’s why I’m very tenacious when
working with writers before taking it out

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:38.470
because I know what the odds are, I mean obviously
a lot of writers know the odds but I see writers

00:02:38.470 --> 00:02:43.989
say “I see stuff get made all the time that’s
not very good.”

00:02:43.989 --> 00:02:49.290
We don’t know the circumstances of how and
why that project got made.

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:53.609
If it went through too many revisions and
lost its center.

00:02:53.609 --> 00:02:57.420
If it went through different regime changes.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:04.370
Nobody starts out trying to make a bad movie…with
exception to one that takes pride in it and

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:10.659
it’s been a huge hit and a cult hit because
there are two movies (I can reference one)

00:03:10.659 --> 00:03:16.829
THE DISASTER ARTIST…well but even that guy
didn’t start out to make a bad movie and

00:03:16.829 --> 00:03:24.049
it obviously began a cult following because
of its uniqueness that way.

00:03:24.049 --> 00:03:32.379
But when people say “There are writers who
get stuff made that are not very good,”

00:03:32.379 --> 00:03:37.459
I feel that’s such a poor model to even
reference.

00:03:37.459 --> 00:03:39.629
So should that be your model?

00:03:39.629 --> 00:03:42.329
“Well, my movie doesn’t have to be perfect.

00:03:42.329 --> 00:03:43.709
I don’t care if it’s that great.

00:03:43.709 --> 00:03:45.300
Other peoples’ can get made.”

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:52.569
No, my feeling is that you need to make sure
that it’s the absolutely best level it can

00:03:52.569 --> 00:03:59.409
be and if you do get consistent feedback where
people are saying “I’m just not loving

00:03:59.409 --> 00:04:05.440
the character enough,” then I think you
need to go back to your script and then talk

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:10.500
to your buddies in the industry whose opinions
you respect and say “Do me a favor.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:16.810
Will you take a look at this again and let
me know maybe what I can make better?

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:20.280
Maybe go in to workshop it?”

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:27.770
Definitely do the research to see…I think
there are some workshops that are better than

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:28.770
others.

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:34.440
And you can do your due diligence by talking
to other writers who have maybe taken a particular

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:38.060
workshop, what they’ve gotten out of it,
what the results have been like and/or of

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:42.949
course worked with the script consultant,
other industry colleagues.

00:04:42.949 --> 00:04:49.030
Like I mentioned if you’re just starting
out you may know assistants who work for agents

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:53.780
or production companies or studios and have
them read it and give you feedback.

